WEBVTT

00:00.480 --> 00:01.430
- [Instructor] This is a screenshot

00:01.430 --> 00:03.160
from an exercise on Khan Academy,

00:03.160 --> 00:07.800
and it says the intergraphic,
the interactive graph below

00:07.800 --> 00:10.030
contains the graph of y is equal

00:10.030 --> 00:13.370
to log base two of x as a dashed curve,

00:13.370 --> 00:15.430
and you can see it down
there as a dashed curve,

00:15.430 --> 00:17.390
with the points one comma zero

00:17.390 --> 00:20.100
and two comma one highlighted.

00:20.100 --> 00:23.660
Adjust the movable
graph to draw y is equal

00:23.660 --> 00:28.660
to four times log base two
of x plus six minus seven.

00:29.020 --> 00:31.920
And so if you happen to have
this exercise in front of you

00:31.920 --> 00:32.840
I encourage you to do that.

00:32.840 --> 00:34.950
Or if you're just thinking
about in your head,

00:34.950 --> 00:36.420
think about how you would approach this.

00:36.420 --> 00:39.160
And I'll give you a hint,
to go from our original

00:39.160 --> 00:42.840
y is equal to log base
two of x to all of this,

00:42.840 --> 00:46.270
it's really going to be a
series of transformations.

00:46.270 --> 00:48.680
And on this tool right over here,

00:48.680 --> 00:51.990
what we can do is we can move
this vertical asymptote around

00:51.990 --> 00:53.100
so that's one thing we can move,

00:53.100 --> 00:57.730
and then we can also
move two of these points.

00:57.730 --> 01:00.147
So where we're starting is right,

01:00.147 --> 01:02.490
we are starting right over there.

01:02.490 --> 01:05.060
And so let's see, and
that was just the graph

01:05.060 --> 01:08.770
of y is equal to log base two of x.

01:08.770 --> 01:11.080
So let's just do these
transformations one at a time.

01:11.080 --> 01:13.090
So the first thing I am going to do,

01:13.090 --> 01:15.020
instead of just doing log base two of x,

01:15.020 --> 01:20.020
let's do log base two of x plus six.

01:20.020 --> 01:22.620
So if you replace your
x with an x plus six,

01:22.620 --> 01:24.040
what is it going to do?

01:24.040 --> 01:27.590
Well it's going to shift
everything six to the left,

01:27.590 --> 01:29.680
and if that doesn't make
intuitive sense to you,

01:29.680 --> 01:30.800
I encourage you to watch some

01:30.800 --> 01:33.890
of the introductory videos
on shifting transformations.

01:33.890 --> 01:36.250
So everything is going
to shift six to the left.

01:36.250 --> 01:39.170
So this vertical asymptote is
going to shift six to the left

01:39.170 --> 01:41.860
it's gonna be, instead of
being at x equals zero,

01:41.860 --> 01:45.440
it's going to go all the way
to x equals negative six.

01:45.440 --> 01:48.370
This point right over here,
which was at one comma zero,

01:48.370 --> 01:49.920
it's going to go six to the left,

01:49.920 --> 01:53.870
one, two, three, four, five, six.

01:53.870 --> 01:56.400
And this point, which as at two comma one,

01:56.400 --> 01:58.660
is gonna go six to the left,

01:58.660 --> 02:03.660
one, two, three, four, five, and six.

02:03.990 --> 02:05.760
So so far what we have graphed

02:05.760 --> 02:10.120
is log base two of x plus six.

02:10.120 --> 02:12.040
So the next thing we might wanna do is

02:12.040 --> 02:17.040
what is four time log
base two of x plus six.

02:17.310 --> 02:18.880
And I want you to think about it is

02:18.880 --> 02:21.690
whatever y-value we were getting before,

02:21.690 --> 02:24.010
we're now going to get four times that.

02:24.010 --> 02:26.470
So when x is equal to negative five,

02:26.470 --> 02:27.860
we're getting a y-value of zero,

02:27.860 --> 02:29.350
but four times zero is still zero,

02:29.350 --> 02:31.140
so that point will stay the same.

02:31.140 --> 02:33.120
But when x is equal to negative four,

02:33.120 --> 02:35.080
we're getting a y-value of one,

02:35.080 --> 02:37.380
but now that's going to
be four times higher,

02:37.380 --> 02:38.710
'cause we're putting that four out front,

02:38.710 --> 02:40.140
so instead of being at four,

02:40.140 --> 02:44.240
instead of being at one
it's going to be at four.

02:44.240 --> 02:45.600
So this right over here is the graph

02:45.600 --> 02:49.070
of y is equal to log
base two of x plus six.

02:49.070 --> 02:50.910
And then the last thing
we have to consider

02:50.910 --> 02:52.740
is well we're gonna take all of that

02:52.740 --> 02:55.090
and then we're going to subtract seven

02:55.090 --> 02:57.800
to get to our target graph.

02:57.800 --> 02:59.570
So whatever points we are here,

02:59.570 --> 03:02.040
we are now going to subtract seven.

03:02.040 --> 03:03.890
So this is at y equals zero,

03:03.890 --> 03:06.000
but now we're going to subtract
seven, so we're going to

03:06.000 --> 03:11.000
go down one, two, three,
four, five, six, seven,

03:11.520 --> 03:13.680
I went off the screen a
little bit, but let me see

03:13.680 --> 03:18.680
if I can scroll down a little
bit so that you can see that,

03:18.760 --> 03:20.990
almost, there you go, now you can see.

03:20.990 --> 03:22.940
I moved this down from
zero to negative seven,

03:22.940 --> 03:24.920
and then this one I
have to move down seven,

03:24.920 --> 03:29.920
one, two, three, four,
five, six, and seven,

03:31.758 --> 03:34.060
and we're done, there you have it.

03:34.060 --> 03:39.060
That is the graph of y is equal
to four times log base two

03:40.170 --> 03:45.170
of x plus six minus
seven, and we are done.
